`ErrorBoundary.tsx` created. Here's what it does:

**Core behaviour**
- `getDerivedStateFromError` captures the error into state, triggering the fallback render
- `componentDidCatch` logs `error.message`, `error.stack`, and React's `componentStack` via `console.error`
- `reset()` clears the error state, re-mounting children — the retry mechanism

**Props**

| Prop | Type | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `children` | `ReactNode` | The tree to protect |
| `fallback` | `(error, reset) => ReactNode` | Custom fallback UI; receives the error and the reset callback |
| `onError` | `(error, info) => void` | Hook for external loggers (Sentry, Datadog, etc.) |

**Usage**

```tsx
// Default fallback
<ErrorBoundary>
  <MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>

// Custom fallback
<ErrorBoundary
  fallback={(err, retry) => (
    <div>
      <p>{err.message}</p>
      <button onClick={retry}>Try again</button>
    </div>
  )}
  onError={(err, info) => Sentry.captureException(err, { extra: info })}
>
  <MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

Note: error boundaries only catch errors in the **render / lifecycle** phase of child components — not async callbacks or event handlers.
